@event hint_ashwood_bite
# en: It Bit Me
title [d2f453] = 它咬了我
# en: Mir catches you in the courtyard sucking a finger, which is smoking slightly. Under their other arm is something long, wrapped in a cloth. "Don't touch that," they say, round the finger. "The ash one. It bit me. I made it and it bit me! It's the best thing I've ever made, probably. I'm not selling it to just anyone."
text [c66804] = 米尔在庭院里拦住你，嘴里含着一根手指，那根手指还微微冒着烟。TA另一只胳膊底下夹着一样长长的东西，用布裹着。“别碰那个，”TA含着手指说，“白蜡木那根。它咬了我。我做的，它咬我！这是我做过最好的东西，大概吧。我可不随便卖给谁。”
# en: A castle courtyard, a young artificer sucking a burnt finger, a long shape wrapped in cloth under their other arm giving off a thin curl of smoke.
scene [4ffcab] = 城堡庭院里，一个年轻的奇械师含着被烫伤的手指，另一只胳膊底下夹着一样用布裹着的长东西，冒出一缕细细的烟。
# en: Find them the burn salve
opt.left.label [79e165] = 去给TA找烫伤药膏
# en: You run to the infirmary for salve and dress the finger on the fountain rim while Mir explains, at length, what an ashwood wand does to anything that tries to block it. "It's at the stall, under a cloth," they say. "If it likes you, it's yours." The next hour goes in bandages and exclamation marks.
opt.left.say.1 [5264ee] = 你跑去医务室拿来药膏，在喷泉池沿上给那根手指上药包扎，米尔则滔滔不绝地讲，一根白蜡木魔杖会对任何想挡住它的东西做什么。“它在摊子上，盖着布，”TA说，“它要是喜欢你，就归你了。”接下来的一个钟头，全是绷带和感叹号。
# en: Ask what makes it bite
opt.right.label [e6d51a] = 问它为什么咬人
# en: Mir lifts a corner of the cloth, then thinks better of it. "Dense grain," they say. "Lightning in the heartwood, years ago, probably. It goes for whatever's in the way." They wrap it again, a little hurt you didn't ask about the finger. "It'll be at the stall. Under a cloth. If you can hold it."
opt.right.say.1 [7df6b6] = 米尔掀起布的一角，又改了主意。“木纹密，”TA说，“好些年前心材里遭过雷击，大概吧。挡路的东西它都要咬。”TA重新裹好，有点受伤，因为你没问那根手指。“它会在摊子上。盖着布。只要你拿得住。”

@event hint_ward_thread
# en: Thread That Shines
title [678e66] = 会发光的线
# en: Sister Amaranth stops you in the corridor, a spool of thread in her hand. It shines, faintly, but only when you aren't looking straight at it. "Ward thread," she says, already turning to go. "Fire slides off it. Frost too. I've sent a spool down to the tailor. For you. Don't argue."
text [26c735] = 阿玛兰斯修女在走廊里拦住你，手里拿着一轴线。线微微发光，但只在你不正眼看它的时候。“护界线，”她说，人已经转身要走，“火从上面滑开。霜也是。我送了一轴到裁缝那儿。给你的。别争。”
# en: A castle corridor, a healer stopping a student with a needle in her collar and a spool of thread in her hand that glimmers faintly from the corner of the eye.
scene [efcdf7] = 城堡走廊里，一位治疗师拦住一个学生，衣领上别着一根针，手里一轴线从眼角看去隐隐发光。
# en: Thank her properly
opt.left.label [e67441] = 好好谢谢她
# en: "Don't," she says, which means she heard. "The tailor in the square. A cloak. You'll pay for the hemming; I won't." Then she sends you away with a list of things you're doing wrong with your sleep.
opt.left.say.1 [9c8b97] = “别谢，”她说，意思是她听见了。“广场上的裁缝。一件斗篷。缝边的钱你出；我不出。”然后她打发你走了，还附带一张你在睡眠上犯的错的清单。
# en: "Why me, Sister?"
opt.right.label [d2881c] = “为什么是我，修女？”
# en: "Because I'm tired of sewing you up," she says. "Square. The tailor. Pay for the hemming." She puts the spool in her pocket. That's all you get, and it's plainly all she means to give.
opt.right.say.1 [5a0551] = “因为我缝你缝累了，”她说，“广场。裁缝。缝边的钱你付。”她把线轴放进口袋。你就得到这么多，显然她也只打算给这么多。

@event hint_almanac_page
# en: Page Forty
title [cf0b92] = 第四十页
# en: A barge hand on the jetty, coiling rope, tells you there's a spring tide on Thursday. "Grimsby's almanac says so," he says. "Page forty. Fifty years of tides in that thing, in his own pencil. He'll not sell it, mind. Not to anyone who doesn't know what it's for."
text [5e29a4] = 码头上一个驳船工一边盘绳一边告诉你，星期四有大潮。“格林斯比的年历上这么写的，”他说，“第四十页。那本东西里有五十年的潮汐，全是他亲笔用铅笔记的。不过他可不卖。不卖给不知道它是干什么用的人。”
# en: A village jetty in the afternoon, a barge hand coiling rope and talking over his shoulder to a student, the bookshop's crooked sign behind them.
scene [6275d4] = 午后的村庄码头，一个驳船工一边盘绳一边回头跟学生说话，身后是书店歪斜的招牌。
# en: Ask what it's for
opt.left.label [8fe944] = 问它是干什么用的
# en: "Tides, moons, weather," says the barge hand. "And something else he won't say. You'd have to ask him." He gives the rope a last tug. "Talk to him about the right thing and he'll give you the shirt off his back. Trouble is knowing what the right thing is."
opt.left.say.1 [241275] = “潮汐、月相、天气，”驳船工说，“还有别的，他不肯说。你得去问他。”他最后拽了一下绳子。“跟他聊对了话题，他连身上的衬衫都肯给你。难就难在知道什么才是对的话题。”
# en: Help him with the rope
opt.right.label [be5065] = 帮他盘绳
# en: You take the other end and he talks all the way through it: about Grimsby, and his almanac, and how the old man keeps it in his waistcoat pocket and pats it like a cat. "Wouldn't sell it to the headmistress herself," he says, "unless she knew what it was for."
opt.right.say.1 [5fdbd8] = 你拉住绳子另一头，他一路说个不停：说格林斯比，说他的年历，说那老头怎么把它揣在背心口袋里，像拍猫一样拍着它。“院长亲自来他都不卖，”他说，“除非她知道那是干什么用的。”

@event hint_spoon_brass
# en: The Old Spoon
title [d43e50] = 那把旧勺子
# en: One of Pell's daughters falls into step with you across the square, a basket of bottles on her arm. "He's retiring the spoon," she says. "His brass one. He says he's getting a new one, and he's been saying it for years. The old one can only go to someone who counts aloud, he says. Nobody counts aloud."
text [24e4fe] = 佩尔的一个女儿挎着一篮瓶子，和你一起穿过广场。“他要让那把勺子退休了，”她说，“他那把黄铜的。他说要换把新的，这话他说了好多年了。他说旧的只能给会大声数数的人。谁会大声数数啊。”
# en: A village square, a young woman in an apothecary's apron rolling her eyes as she talks, a basket of corked bottles on her arm.
scene [2b2c4a] = 村庄广场上，一个系着药铺围裙的年轻女人边说边翻白眼，胳膊上挎着一篮塞着软木塞的瓶子。
# en: "I count aloud."
opt.left.label [52ec28] = “我会大声数。”
# en: She looks at you sideways, then makes you count the bottles in her basket aloud, twice, in the middle of the square. "Then go and do that in front of him," she says. "Bring money: he'll want it down first, and he'll give it back if you lose count." She sighs. "You won't lose count. He's never wrong about people."
opt.left.say.1 [542af5] = 她斜眼看了看你，然后让你当着整个广场，把她篮子里的瓶子大声数了两遍。“那就去他面前这么数，”她说，“带上钱：他要你先付，数错了会还给你。”她叹了口气，“你不会数错的。他看人从来不会错。”
# en: Carry her basket for her
opt.right.label [2f566b] = 帮她提篮子
# en: You carry the bottles to the jetty, which is heavier than it looks, while she tells you all about the spoon, and the drops, and her father. "Count aloud," she says at the barge. "In front of him. That's the whole secret. Don't tell him I told you."
opt.right.say.1 [c3b4d3] = 你把瓶子一路提到码头，比看上去重得多，她一路跟你讲那把勺子、那些药滴，还有她父亲。“大声数，”她在驳船边说，“当着他的面。秘诀就这一个。别告诉他是我说的。”

@event hint_silver_honey
# en: Bread and Silver
title [931059] = 面包与银
# en: Old Maud is eating a late lunch on an upturned pot in the greenhouses, and holds out a second slice of bread without asking. The honey on it shines like poured silver. "Silver bees," she says. "Mine. They don't take to everyone, dearie. Come by the cottage when I'm doing the frames, and there's a tin in it."
text [1c5aa6] = 老莫德坐在温室里一个倒扣的花盆上吃一顿迟来的午饭，没问一声就递给你第二片面包。上面的蜂蜜亮得像倒出来的银子。“银蜂，”她说，“我的。它们可不是跟谁都合得来，孩子。等我收蜂框的时候到小屋来，有你一罐。”
# en: A warm greenhouse in the afternoon, an old woman in a gardening apron holding out a slice of bread spread with honey that shines like poured silver.
scene [01c464] = 午后温暖的温室里，一位系着园艺围裙的老妇人递出一片面包，上面抹的蜂蜜亮得像倒出来的银子。
# en: Eat it there and then
opt.left.label [3dd408] = 当场就吃
# en: It tastes of something you can't name: clover, and cold mornings, and a little of the lake. Maud watches you eat it with great satisfaction and tells you, at length, about a bee who once followed her to church.
opt.left.say.1 [724a9e] = 它的味道你说不上来：三叶草，清冷的早晨，还有一点点湖水。莫德心满意足地看着你吃，然后絮絮叨叨地给你讲了一只蜜蜂跟着她进教堂的事。
# en: Wrap it for later
opt.right.label [918ffb] = 包起来留着
# en: You wrap it in your handkerchief and eat it that evening, when you're flagging, and feel better at once. The handkerchief keeps a faint silver stain that never quite washes out. Maud, when you see her next, asks if you ate it, and looks a little hurt when you say you saved it.
opt.right.say.1 [5be25f] = 你用手帕包好，到了晚上没精神的时候才吃，一下子就好多了。手帕上留下了一块淡淡的银色印子，怎么也洗不掉。下次见到莫德，她问你吃了没有，你说留着了，她看上去有点受伤。

@event hint_heartwood_warm
# en: Warm in Your Bag
title [558eb8] = 包里的温热
# en: The splinter of heartwood the elder oak gave you is still in your bag, months on, and still warm, like a hand that's just let go of yours. Your books have started to smell of the forest. Mir, at the market stall, would know what to do with it.
text [589461] = 老橡树给你的那根心木木片还在你包里，过了几个月，依然是温的，像一只刚松开你的手。你的书开始有森林的味道了。集市摊位上的米尔，会知道拿它怎么办。
# en: A satchel open on a dormitory bed, a red-gold splinter of wood inside it glowing faintly warm among the books.
scene [98bd5d] = 宿舍床上一个打开的书包，书本之间一根红金色的木片泛着微微的暖光。
# en: Take it to Mir
opt.left.label [8b084c] = 拿去给米尔
# en: You put it in your pocket for the next time you're in the village. It's warm against your leg all day, and you find yourself walking towards the square without quite deciding to.
opt.left.say.1 [eb5299] = 你把它揣进口袋，等下次去村里。它贴着你的腿暖了一整天，你发现自己不知不觉就朝广场走去了。
# en: Keep it a while longer
opt.right.label [6a9a4c] = 再留一阵子
# en: You put it on your windowsill instead. In the morning a sprig of green has come out of one end, very small. You decide it isn't in a hurry, and neither are you.
opt.right.say.1 [9ec16d] = 你把它放到窗台上。第二天早上，它一头冒出了一点点绿芽，非常小。你认定它不着急，你也不着急。

@event hint_silk_light
# en: Light Goes In
title [58fa7a] = 光照进去就不出来
# en: The Spinner's silk is folded in the bottom of your trunk. When you open the lid tonight, the candle beside it bends towards the grey, and dims, as if the silk were drinking it. The village tailor would know what to do with something like this. Possibly she'd be frightened of it.
text [f49781] = 织者的丝叠放在你箱子的最底下。今晚你打开箱盖时，旁边的蜡烛朝那片灰色弯了过去，暗了下来，像是被丝吸走了。村里的裁缝会知道拿这种东西怎么办。也可能她会怕它。
# en: A dormitory trunk lid raised in candlelight, a bolt of grey silk inside, the candle beside it guttering as if in a draught that isn't there.
scene [695309] = 烛光里掀开的宿舍箱盖，里面一匹灰色的丝，旁边的蜡烛摇曳不定，像是有一阵并不存在的风。
# en: Take it to the tailor soon
opt.left.label [ae6da9] = 尽快拿给裁缝
# en: You wrap it in a pillowcase for the walk down. Even through the cloth your hand feels cool where it holds it. The candle stands up straight again as soon as the trunk is shut.
opt.left.say.1 [b584b6] = 你用枕套把它包好，准备带下山。隔着布，握着它的那只手也觉得凉。箱子一合上，蜡烛就又直直地立了起来。
# en: Shut the trunk on it
opt.right.label [6e0028] = 把它关回箱子里
# en: You shut the lid. The candle straightens. You sleep with your back to the trunk and dream of very fine threads, and of someone patient at the end of them.
opt.right.say.1 [5baba1] = 你合上箱盖。蜡烛直了起来。你背对着箱子睡觉，梦见了极细极细的丝线，还有线的那一头一个很有耐心的人。
